</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 9, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We explicitly construct Brill–Noether general <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4,6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4,6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> having the maximal number of elliptic pencils of degrees <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3,4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and study their moduli spaces and moduli maps to the moduli space of curves.
As an application we prove the existence of Brill–Noether general <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> without stable Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles of minimal <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>K3 surfaces, Unirationality, Moduli map, Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundle
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2010 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>14J28, 51M15, (14Q10, 14J10) 
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is well known that a general curve of genus <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 9</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=11</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized as a linear section of a primitively polarized <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk88</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk96</a>]</cite>. Since for even <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> a general curve <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> carries a finite number of pencils of minimal degree <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{g}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{g}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, it is natural to ask whether one can simultaneously extend <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and all or some of these pencils to some <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces for <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4,6,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4,6,8</annotation></semantics></math>.
This question is connected to the existence of non-stable Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles. Indeed, the Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundle associated to a pencil on a smooth curve on the <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface induced by an elliptic pencil on the surface is necessarily not stable, cf. Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmdefinition1" title="Lemma 5.1. ‣ 5. Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles and their stability ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using vector bundle methods, Mukai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk02</a>]</cite> showed that the projective model of any <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Brill–Noether general</em> <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,L)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained as sections of homogeneous varieties for <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\{6,\dots,10,12\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\{6,\dots,10,12\}</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk02</a>, Def. 3.8]</cite>, a polarized <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,L)</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is Brill–Noether general if <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(M)h^{0}(N)&lt;g+1=h^{0}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(M)h^{0}(N)&lt;g+1=h^{0}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> for any non-trivial decomposition <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\sim M+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\sim M+N</annotation></semantics></math>. In these low genera this is equivalent to all the smooth curves in the linear system <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|L|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|L|</annotation></semantics></math> being Brill–Noether general, due to techniques in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laz86</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">GL87</a>]</cite> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLT15</a>, Lemma 1.7]</cite>).
Using Mukai’s results, we will study projective models of Brill–Noether general <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\{4,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\{4,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math> containing the maximal possible number of elliptic pencils of degree <math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{g}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{g}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of our paper is threefold:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide explicit constructions/equations of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces with special geometric features.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe their moduli spaces as lattice polarized <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces and the corresponding moduli map to the moduli space of curves of genus <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the slope-stability of Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles of hyperplane sections on such <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our main results are the following.

<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> §<a href="#S3" title="3. 
        
          
            ⁢
            K
            3
          
        
       surfaces of genus 
        
          4 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>: We prove that a general curve <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear section of a smooth <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> such that its two <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>s (which are well-known to be auto-residual) are induced by two elliptic pencils <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\sim E_{1}+E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\sim E_{1}+E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3.1. K3 surfaces of genus 4 with an elliptic pencil of degree 3 ‣ 3. 
        
          
            ⁢
            K
            3
          
        
       surfaces of genus 
        
          4 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. Furthermore, the moduli space parametrizing such <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces is unirational (and <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional), cf. Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.1. K3 surfaces of genus 4 with an elliptic pencil of degree 3 ‣ 3. 
        
          
            ⁢
            K
            3
          
        
       surfaces of genus 
        
          4 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. We believe that these results should be known, but could not find any reference.

<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> §<a href="#S4" title="4. K3 surfaces of genus 6 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>:
A general curve <math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> carries precisely five pencils <math id="S1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A_{1}|,\dots,|A_{5}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A_{1}|,\dots,|A_{5}|</annotation></semantics></math> of minimal degree <math id="S1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfy <math id="S1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2K_{C}\sim A_{1}+\dots+A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2K_{C}\sim A_{1}+\dots+A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGH85</a>, p. 209ff]</cite>). We prove that <math id="S1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear section of a smooth <math id="S1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> such that its five <math id="S1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>s are induced by five elliptic pencils <math id="S1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{1}|,\dots,|E_{5}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{1}|,\dots,|E_{5}|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S1.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2C\sim E_{1}+\dots+E_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2C\sim E_{1}+\dots+E_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, cf Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4.1. K3 sections of a cone of the Grassmannian 
          
            
              G
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    V
                    5
                  
                  ,
                  2
                  ) ‣ 4. K3 surfaces of genus 6 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>(a). We prove that the moduli space parametrizing such pairs <math id="S1.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,C)</annotation></semantics></math> is unirational, cf. Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4.1. K3 sections of a cone of the Grassmannian 
          
            
              G
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    V
                    5
                  
                  ,
                  2
                  ) ‣ 4. K3 surfaces of genus 6 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>(b). The moduli space of the underlying <math id="S1.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces was already studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK11</a>]</cite> where it was shown to be birational to the moduli space <math id="S1.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> of curves of genus <math id="S1.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> (whence rational, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">SB89</a>]</cite>). Our approach shows that this moduli space is exactly the locus of Brill–Noether general <math id="S1.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces that cannot be realized as quadratic sections of a smooth quintic Del Pezzo threefold (but as quadratic sections of a cone over a smooth quintic Del Pezzo surface), cf. Remark <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition4" title="Remark 4.4. ‣ 4.1. K3 sections of a cone of the Grassmannian 
          
            
              G
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    V
                    5
                  
                  ,
                  2
                  ) ‣ 4. K3 surfaces of genus 6 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>(b).

<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S1.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> §<a href="#S6" title="6. K3 surfaces of genus 8 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>:
A general curve <math id="S1.p4.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.p4.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> carries precisely <math id="S1.p4.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math> pencils of degree <math id="S1.p4.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. An easy lattice computation shows that at most <math id="S1.p4.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended to a <math id="S1.p4.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface containing <math id="S1.p4.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. We prove that this bound is reached in codimension <math id="S1.p4.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> in the moduli space <math id="S1.p4.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{8}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for a general curve only six out of its <math id="S1.p4.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math> pencils can be extended to elliptic pencils on a <math id="S1.p4.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface, cf. Corollary <a href="#S6.Thmdefinition11" title="Corollary 6.11. ‣ 6.3. The moduli map ‣ 6. K3 surfaces of genus 8 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.11</span></a>.
We prove that the moduli spaces of such <math id="S1.p4.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces containing <math id="S1.p4.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> elliptic pencils are unirational for <math id="S1.p4.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=9</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Theorems <a href="#S6.Thmdefinition7" title="Theorem 6.7. ‣ 6.2. A unirational construction of 
          
            
              ⁢
              K
              3
            
          
         surfaces with nine distinct elliptic pencils ‣ 6. K3 surfaces of genus 8 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a> and <a href="#S6.Thmdefinition8" title="Theorem 6.8. ‣ 6.2. A unirational construction of 
          
            
              ⁢
              K
              3
            
          
         surfaces with nine distinct elliptic pencils ‣ 6. K3 surfaces of genus 8 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.8</span></a>.

<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S1.p4.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> §<a href="#S5" title="5. Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles and their stability ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>: The <math id="S1.p4.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces constructed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. 
        
          
            ⁢
            K
            3
          
        
       surfaces of genus 
        
          4 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (respectively <a href="#S4" title="4. K3 surfaces of genus 6 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) provide examples of <math id="S1.p4.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces without stable (resp. semistable) Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles with <math id="S1.p4.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}=3</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S1.p4.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>), cf. Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmdefinition2" title="Corollary 5.2. ‣ 5. Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles and their stability ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> (resp. <a href="#S5.Thmdefinition3" title="Corollary 5.3. ‣ 5. Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles and their stability ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>). This shows in particular the sharpness of a result of Lelli-Chiesa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">LC13</a>, Thm. 4.3]</cite>, cf. Remark <a href="#S5.Thmdefinition4" title="Remark 5.4. ‣ 5. Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles and their stability ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Notation and conventions</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We work over <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will denote <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector space and <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(k,V_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(k,V_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{n},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{n},k)</annotation></semantics></math>) the Grassmannian of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional sub- (resp. quotient-) spaces of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The projective space of one-dimensional sub- (resp. quotient-) spaces is denoted <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}_{*}(V_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}_{*}(V_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{*}(V_{n}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{*}(V_{n}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors benefitted from conversations with Christian Bopp and Frank-Olaf Schreyer and acknowledge support from grant n. 261756 of the Research Council of Norway.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Lattice polarized <math id="S2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces and their moduli spaces</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a lattice. The moduli space <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>-polarized <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces parametrizes pairs <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\varphi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\varphi)</annotation></semantics></math> (up to isomorphism) consisting of a <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and a primitive lattice embedding <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:{\mathfrak{h}}\to\mathop{\rm Pic}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔥</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Pic</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:{\mathfrak{h}}\to\mathop{\rm Pic}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi({\mathfrak{h}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔥</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi({\mathfrak{h}})</annotation></semantics></math> contains an ample class. It is a quasi-projective irreducible <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(20-\mathop{\rm rk}({\mathfrak{h}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">rk</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔥</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(20-\mathop{\rm rk}({\mathfrak{h}}))</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional variety by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dol96</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\varphi)\in{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\varphi)\in{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>-polarized <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface and <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in{\mathfrak{h}}\cong\varphi({\mathfrak{h}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔥</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔥</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in{\mathfrak{h}}\cong\varphi({\mathfrak{h}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a distinguished class with <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}=2g-2\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}=2g-2\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>,
one may consider the open subset</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}_{g}=\left\{(S,\varphi)\ \big|\ (S,\varphi)\in{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}\text{ and }L\text{ ample }\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> ample </mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}_{g}=\left\{(S,\varphi)\ \big|\ (S,\varphi)\in{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}\text{ and }L\text{ ample }\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">of the moduli space <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may also be considered as a subset of the moduli space <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of polarized <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, let
<math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{\mathfrak{h}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{\mathfrak{h}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the moduli space of triples <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\varphi,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\varphi,C)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in|L|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in|L|</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth irreducible curve in the distinguished linear system.
Then we have moduli maps</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{g}:{\mathcal{P}}^{\mathfrak{h}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{g}:{\mathcal{P}}^{\mathfrak{h}}_{g}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Since in our cases of study it will be clear what the distinguished class <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> will be, we will often skip the index <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝔥</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}_{g}^{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span><math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="S3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will show the unirationality of the moduli space <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> of lattice polarized <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces where <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the hyperbolic lattice of rank <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. We believe that this result should be well-known, but we could not find any reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following example is well-known, but we include it for the sake of the reader and it serves as an introduction for our next results and constructions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The moduli space of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A smooth polarized <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete intersection of a quadric <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and a cubic hypersurface <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. The quadric <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=V(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=V(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and the cubic <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=V(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=V(y)</annotation></semantics></math> are given by polynomials <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(2))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(2))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(3))</annotation></semantics></math> of degrees <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The moduli space <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> is described as follows. The quadric has to be of rank at least <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> since otherwise <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> will be singular. Let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(2))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(2))</annotation></semantics></math> be the open subset consisting of quadratic equations of rank <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. For a chosen equation <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> we need to pick a cubic <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is no multiple of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and the intersection of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> should be smooth. Let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the five-codimensional quotient of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(3))</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing non-multiples of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. The desired cubic equations are parametrized by an open subset <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}\subset V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}\subset V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be the iterated Grassmannian</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.Ex1.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="29.37" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-11.069600110696001px" version="1.1" viewBox="11.65 11.07 179.36 29.37" width="179.36"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 11.646148820990296 29.36517745277043) translate(0,3.46) translate(11.65,0)"><g transform="translate(-11.65,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.08em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="14.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{W\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{W\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 11.646148820990296 -0.27673578005007266 L 11.646148820990296 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(26.38,0) translate(0,9.15) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.77em;--ltx-fo-height:0.53em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.99" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.26)" width="65.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{G(1,W_{q})\ \ \ \ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">q</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.64em;" width="1.64em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{G(1,W_{q})\ \ \ \ }</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(86.37,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 11.646148820990296 -0.27673578005007266 L 86.3659495681883 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 86.3659495681883 -0.27673578005007266 L 86.3659495681883 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(86.37,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.28em;--ltx-fo-height:0.81em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.72" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.26)" width="73.05"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\mathbb{P}}_{*}(V)\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{14}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>14</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\mathbb{P}}_{*}(V)\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{14}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">whose fibers are Grassmannians of one-dimensional subspaces of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is birational to <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> modulo the automorphism group of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, whence <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is unirational. Note further that a dimension count yields</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim V+\dim W_{q}-\dim PGL(5)=\left(\binom{6}{2}-1\right)+\left(\binom{7}{3}-1-5\right)-(5^{2}-1)=19," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>6</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>7</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim V+\dim W_{q}-\dim PGL(5)=\left(\binom{6}{2}-1\right)+\left(\binom{7}{3}-1-5\right)-(5^{2}-1)=19,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">as expected.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>K3 surfaces of genus 4 with an elliptic pencil of degree 3</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With notation as in the previous example let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface of genus <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> with polarization <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L={\mathcal{O}}_{S}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L={\mathcal{O}}_{S}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that there exists a class <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathop{\rm Pic}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">∈</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">Pic</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathop{\rm Pic}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E.L=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E.L=3</annotation></semantics></math>. By Riemann–Roch, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(S,E)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(S,E)=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth elliptic normal curve for general <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\in|E|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\in|E|</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we get a pencil of elliptic normal curves. The pencil induces a rational normal scroll</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\bigcup_{E^{\prime}\in|E|}\overline{E^{\prime}}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\bigcup_{E^{\prime}\in|E|}\overline{E^{\prime}}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">of dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and degree <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{E^{\prime}}={\mathbb{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{E^{\prime}}={\mathbb{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the linear span of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the scroll <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique quadric hypersurface containing <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, the scroll <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is singular in a point (since any two different projective planes in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> intersect and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be singular along a line), that is, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> quadric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that the residual class <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-E</annotation></semantics></math> is a second elliptic pencil of degree <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and the maximal number of such pencils is two since <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a unique quadric. We get a <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface whose Picard lattice contains the intersection matrix with respect to the ordered basis <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{L,E\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{L,E\}</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{L-E,E\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{L-E,E\}</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}6&amp;3\\
3&amp;0\end{pmatrix}\left(\mbox{resp. }\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;3\\
3&amp;0\end{pmatrix}={\mathfrak{U}}(3)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>6</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>resp. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}6&amp;3\\
3&amp;0\end{pmatrix}\left(\mbox{resp. }\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;3\\
3&amp;0\end{pmatrix}={\mathfrak{U}}(3)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the hyperbolic lattice of rank <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum of the two basis elements of square <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. In general <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Pic}(S)\cong{\mathfrak{U}}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Pic</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Pic}(S)\cong{\mathfrak{U}}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> (such <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces exist by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor84</a>, Thm. 2.9(i)]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nik80</a>]</cite>),
in which case <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique element (up to sign) of square <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>, whence genus <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, which is easily seen to be very ample by the classical results of Saint-Donat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">SD74</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, such a <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,L)</annotation></semantics></math> is Brill–Noether general.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from the introduction that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{U}}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{U}}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>-polarized <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The moduli space <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> is unirational.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By what we said, a general element in <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> comes equipped with a unique embedding into <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> (up to the action of the projective linear group), as a complete intersection of a cubic and a rank <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> quadric, singular in a point. The converse holds true: if a smooth surface <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete intersection of a rank <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> quadric hypersurface <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and a cubic hypersurface, then the two rulings on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> cut out two residual elliptic pencils of degree <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We describe a birational model of the moduli space <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> by modifying the construction in Example <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="Example 3.1 (The moduli space of 
          
            
              ⁢
              K
              3
            
          
         surfaces of genus 
          
            4
          
        ). ‣ 3. 
        
          
            ⁢
            K
            3
          
        
       surfaces of genus 
        
          4 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, keeping the notation therein.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}\subset H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(2))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}\subset H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{4},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{4}}(2))</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset of quadratic equations of rank <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. Since a rank <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> quadric is a cone over a smooth quadric in <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the space <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to an open subset of a <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{3},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{3}}(2))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{3},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{3}}(2))</annotation></semantics></math> and is therefore unirational. Pick <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in V^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in V^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the moduli space <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> is birational to the iterated Grassmannian</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.Ex5.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="25.91" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-7.610350076103501px" version="1.1" viewBox="13.59 7.61 125.22 25.91" width="125.22"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 13.58716306769536 25.905927418177928) translate(0,3.46) translate(13.59,0)"><g transform="translate(-13.59,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="18.87"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{W^{\prime}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{W^{\prime}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 13.587184181281998 -0.27673578005007266 L 13.587184181281998 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(18.51,0) translate(0,9.15) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.95em;--ltx-fo-height:0.53em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.99" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.26)" width="54.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{G(1,W_{q})\ \ }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">q</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{G(1,W_{q})\ \ }</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(88.31,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 13.587184181281998 -0.27673578005007266 L 88.30696381489336 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 88.30696381489336 -0.27673578005007266 L 88.30696381489336 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(88.31,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.09em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.4" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="15.03"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{V^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{V^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">modulo automorphisms and is therefore unirational, too. (Since <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim V^{\prime}=\binom{5}{2}-1+4=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim V^{\prime}=\binom{5}{2}-1+4=13</annotation></semantics></math>, a dimension count yields that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a codimension one subspace of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, as expected.)
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the hyperbolic lattice of rank <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Even if the example above should be classically known, we only found in the literature unirationality results of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHK16</a>]</cite>). Elliptic surfaces are parametrized by <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔘</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and double covers of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{1}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{1}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> branched along a curve of bidegree <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4,4)</annotation></semantics></math> are parametrized by <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from the introduction that <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space of triples <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\varphi,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\varphi,C)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\varphi)\in{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\varphi)\in{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in|L|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in|L|</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth curve of genus <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> in the distinguished linear system.
Also recall that a general curve of genus <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> has exactly two distinct <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>s, which are auto-residual.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The moduli map <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is dominant. In particular, a general curve <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear section of a smooth <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> such that its two <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>s are induced by two elliptic pencils <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\sim E_{1}+E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\sim E_{1}+E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We consider a general curve <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, canonically embedded into <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a complete intersection of a smooth quadric <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and a cubic <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (the quadric <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth since the two <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>s are distinct). We will construct a <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> with the curve <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> as a linear section. Therefore, we choose a <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> containing the ambient space <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of the curve. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be a cone over the quadric <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, a rank <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> quadric whose hyperplane section with the given <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be any cubic hypersurface such that <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cap{\mathbb{P}}^{3}=Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cap{\mathbb{P}}^{3}=Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The surface <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen as the complete intersection of <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the pair <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,C)</annotation></semantics></math> is an element of <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>𝔘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{U}}(3)}</annotation></semantics></math> by construction, and the dominance of the moduli map follows.
The last statement is immediate.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon02</a>]</cite> it is shown that the moduli space of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces admitting a special automorphism of order <math id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> is birational to the moduli space of curves of genus <math id="S3.Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">AS08</a>]</cite> for its generalization).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>K3 surfaces of genus 6</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the seminal work of Mukai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk93</a>]</cite>, we will construct a Brill–Noether general <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> where every complete pencil of degree <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> on a hyperplane section of <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by an elliptic pencil on <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, we show that the moduli space of such lattice polarized <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces is unirational.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly recall Mukai’s construction. Let <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,L)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Brill–Noether general <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface of genus <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. There exists a unique stable (rigid) vector bundle <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})=L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}({\mathcal{E}})=L</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(S,{\mathcal{E}})=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(S,{\mathcal{E}})=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{i}(S,{\mathcal{E}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{i}(S,{\mathcal{E}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">IP99</a>, Prop. 5.2.7]</cite>. This bundle induces an embedding of <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> into the Grassmannian <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{5}=H^{0}(S,{\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{5}=H^{0}(S,{\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math>, by sending <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> to the fiber <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{s}={\mathcal{E}}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{s}={\mathcal{E}}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk93</a>]</cite>, a Brill–Noether general <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the intersection of a linear section of codimension 3 (or 4) and a quadratic section of either the Plücker embedding <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{5},2)\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{5},2)\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math> or of its cone <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In order to get an elliptic pencil of degree <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> on a <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface, we need special sections of the following form. If the linear section of codimension <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> cuts a sub-Grassmannian of type <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(4,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(4,2)</annotation></semantics></math> in a quadric surface, we get an elliptic normal curve of degree <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> as the intersection of this quadric surface with the quadric section. A pencil of Grassmannians of type <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(4,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(4,2)</annotation></semantics></math> induces a pencil of elliptic curves on <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and can be controlled in the dual space in the following way.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A hyperplane corresponds to a point in the dual Grassmannian <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(2,V_{5})\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{9}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow></mrow><mn>9</mn><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(2,V_{5})\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{9}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it cuts out a Schubert subvariety. Moreover, the Schubert variety is a one-dimensional union of Grassmannians of type <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(4,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(4,2)</annotation></semantics></math> contained in <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will prove the same statement for the Grassmannian <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{6},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{6},2)</annotation></semantics></math> in the next section (cf. Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmdefinition1" title="Lemma 6.1 (Mukai). ‣ 6. K3 surfaces of genus 8 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) and leave this proof to the readers. Note that two Grassmannians of type <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(4,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(4,2)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math> intersect in a <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-plane. Hence, two elliptic curves of distinct pencils of degree <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> intersect in two points.
This can also be seen in the following way: if <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are such elliptic curves, then <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}.E_{2}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}.E_{2}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> (as each <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> is a pencil); moreover, since <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L-E_{1})^{2}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L-E_{1})^{2}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, one also has <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4-E_{1}.E_{2}=E_{2}.(L-E_{1})\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4-E_{1}.E_{2}=E_{2}.(L-E_{1})\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, whence <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}.E_{2}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}.E_{2}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Also inspired by the previous example of <math id="S4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="S4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, we will construct a <math id="S4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface with Picard lattice of the following form:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}10&amp;4&amp;4&amp;\dots&amp;4\\
4&amp;0&amp;2&amp;\dots&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;0&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;\dots&amp;2&amp;0\end{pmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>10</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}10&amp;4&amp;4&amp;\dots&amp;4\\
4&amp;0&amp;2&amp;\dots&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;0&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;\dots&amp;2&amp;0\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">An easy computation shows that the rank can be at most five (otherwise the matrix has at least two non-negative eigenvalues).
Let <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the lattice given by the following intersection matrix</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{M}}=\begin{pmatrix}10&amp;4&amp;4&amp;4&amp;4\\
4&amp;0&amp;2&amp;2&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;0&amp;2&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;2&amp;0&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;2&amp;2&amp;0\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔐</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>10</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{M}}=\begin{pmatrix}10&amp;4&amp;4&amp;4&amp;4\\
4&amp;0&amp;2&amp;2&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;0&amp;2&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;2&amp;0&amp;2\\
4&amp;2&amp;2&amp;2&amp;0\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We denote <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface with the above Picard lattice <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> (which exists by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor84</a>, Thm. 2.9(i)]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nik80</a>]</cite>) and let <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be the basis element of square <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,4</annotation></semantics></math>, be the generators of square zero. Note that <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{5}:=2L-E_{1}-E_{2}-E_{3}-E_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{5}:=2L-E_{1}-E_{2}-E_{3}-E_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is also an element of square zero and degree <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The lattice <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also generated by elements <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0},s_{1},\dots,s_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0},s_{1},\dots,s_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}=E_{1}+\cdots+E_{4}-L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}=E_{1}+\cdots+E_{4}-L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=s_{0}-E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=s_{0}-E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,4</annotation></semantics></math>, with intersection matrix</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;-2&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;-2&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;-2&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;-2\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;-2&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;-2&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;-2&amp;0\\
0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;-2\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">(This is the lattice considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK11</a>]</cite>.) We may assume that <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is big and nef by standard arguments (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">BHPV04</a>, VIII, Prop. 3.10]</cite>). Note that <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=3s_{0}-\sum_{i=1}^{4}s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=3s_{0}-\sum_{i=1}^{4}s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}=s_{0}-s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}=s_{0}-s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{5}=6s_{0}-3\sum_{i=1}^{4}s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{5}=6s_{0}-3\sum_{i=1}^{4}s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The class </span><math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is ample.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The </span><math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> surface </span><math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,L)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is Brill–Noether general.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The classes </span><math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},\dots,E_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},\dots,E_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> define elliptic pencils and are the only classes in </span><math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathop{\rm Pic}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">Pic</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathop{\rm Pic}(S)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of square </span><math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and degree </span><math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with respect to </span><math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\sum_{i=0}^{4}a_{i}s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\sum_{i=0}^{4}a_{i}s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary class. Then <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{2}=2a_{0}-2\sum_{i=1}^{4}a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{2}=2a_{0}-2\sum_{i=1}^{4}a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, whence
<math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L.\Delta=8a_{0}-\Delta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L.\Delta=8a_{0}-\Delta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is effective, then <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}=\frac{1}{2}s_{0}.\Delta\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}=\frac{1}{2}s_{0}.\Delta\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is nef.
It follows that <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L.\Delta\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L.\Delta\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-curve <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, whence (a) is proved.
It also immediately follows that there exists no nontrivial effective class <math id="S4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> such that either <math id="S4.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta.L\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta.L\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{2}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{2}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta.L=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta.L=5</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies (b) by either a direct computation using the definition of Brill–Noether generality or invoking, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">JK04</a>, Prop. 10.5]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">SD74</a>]</cite>, or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLT15</a>, Lemma 1.7]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">To prove that <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math> is an elliptic pencil, it suffices to show that <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is nef by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">SD74</a>]</cite>. If <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> is not nef,
there exists a <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-curve <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma.E_{i}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma.E_{i}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k:=-\Gamma.E_{i}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k:=-\Gamma.E_{i}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{i}-k\Gamma)^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{i}-k\Gamma)^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}-k\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}-k\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is effective and nontrivial with <math id="S4.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E_{i}-k\Gamma).L\leq 4-k\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E_{i}-k\Gamma).L\leq 4-k\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math> by ampleness of <math id="S4.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction to the Brill–Noether generality. Finally, if <math id="S4.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is another effective class with <math id="S4.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F.E_{i}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F.E_{i}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S4.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> moves in (at least) a pencil. Thus <math id="S4.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F.L=\frac{1}{2}F.(E_{1}+\dots+E_{5})\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F.L=\frac{1}{2}F.(E_{1}+\dots+E_{5})\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We will show that the general curve lies on a six-dimensional family of such <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of Picard rank <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. We will use the cone over the Grassmannian <math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>K3 sections of a cone of the Grassmannian <math id="S4.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the rank <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> lattice above.
Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the moduli space of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>-polarized <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in the introduction. Recall that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}=15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}=21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}=21</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also recall that a general genus <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> curve carries precisely five elliptic pencils <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A_{1}|,\ldots,|A_{5}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A_{1}|,\ldots,|A_{5}|</annotation></semantics></math> of degree four, which satisfy <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2K_{C}\sim A_{1}+\cdots+A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2K_{C}\sim A_{1}+\cdots+A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK11</a>]</cite> the moduli space <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is birational to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is well-known to be rational by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">SB89</a>]</cite>. More precisely, Artebani and Kondō show that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the locus of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces admitting a double cover to a quintic Del Pezzo surface branched along a curve of genus <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, this shows that the moduli map
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> is dominant since we get a section. However, the pairs <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,L)</annotation></semantics></math> admit automorphisms fixing <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, whence <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not birational to a <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and one cannot conclude its unirationality from the rationality of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will show by our construction that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is unirational and that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of polarized <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> such that all the five <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>s of their smooth curve sections are induced by elliptic pencils on the surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)  The moduli map <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> is dominant. Furthermore, a general curve <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear section of a smooth <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> such that its five <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>s are induced by five elliptic pencils <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E_{1}|,\dots,|E_{5}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E_{1}|,\dots,|E_{5}|</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2C\sim E_{1}+\dots+E_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2C\sim E_{1}+\dots+E_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)  <math id="S4.Thmdefinition3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is unirational.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(a) We will describe a <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface containing the general curve in <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as the geometry describing the elliptic pencils on the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface. This is based on Mukai’s result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk93</a>, §6]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in{\mathcal{M}}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in{\mathcal{M}}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> be a general curve of genus <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> which is given as follows. We fix a Plücker embedding of the Grassmannian <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{5},2)\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{5},2)\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a projective <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-space <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as a quadric hypersurface <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=P\cap Q\cap G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=P\cap Q\cap G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}={\mathbb{P}}^{3}\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{9}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow></mrow><mn>9</mn><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}={\mathbb{P}}^{3}\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{9}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dual space. As <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to be general, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1}_{4}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1}_{4}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is finite-dimensional, more precisely <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1}_{4}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1}_{4}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of five smooth points, and is isomorphic to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}\cap G(2,V_{5})\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{9}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow></mrow><mn>9</mn><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}\cap G(2,V_{5})\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{9}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the intersection of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> and the dual Grassmannian <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(2,V_{5})=G(V_{5},2)^{\vee}\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{9}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow></mrow><mn>9</mn><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(2,V_{5})=G(V_{5},2)^{\vee}\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{9}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4. K3 surfaces of genus 6 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> each point of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}\cap G(2,V_{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}\cap G(2,V_{5})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a pencil of Grassmannians of type <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(4,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(4,2)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math>. This pencil induces a cubic scroll in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>9</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{9}</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> cuts out the corresponding point of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1}_{4}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1}_{4}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now let <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> be the cone over the Grassmannian <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math> with vertex point <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{G(2,V_{5})}\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{10}}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow></mrow><mn>10</mn><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{G(2,V_{5})}\subset{{\mathbb{P}}^{10}}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> the cone over the dual Grassmannian with vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{G(2,V_{5})}=\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{G(2,V_{5})}=\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider the given projective <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-space <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> as a subspace of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{v}=\overline{P+v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{v}=\overline{P+v}</annotation></semantics></math> be the span of <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and the vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}\subset P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}\subset P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> be a quadric hypersurface such that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}\cap P=Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}\cap P=Q</annotation></semantics></math>. We get a <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}\cap P_{v}\cap Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}\cap P_{v}\cap Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we can assume to be smooth for general <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the dual space of this <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the above <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. As above the five intersection points <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}\cap\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}=P^{\vee}\cap G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}\cap\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}=P^{\vee}\cap G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to five pencils of Grassmannians in <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>10</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{10}</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction to <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are the five elliptic pencils of degree <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. We get the desired <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface with the right Picard lattice.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">(b) Recall that any canonical model of a general curve of genus <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized as a quadratic section of a fixed quintic Del Pezzo surface <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">SB89</a>]</cite>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We fix a <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{6}\supset{\mathbb{P}}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>⊃</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{6}\supset{\mathbb{P}}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> and a point <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in{\mathbb{P}}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in{\mathbb{P}}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> be the cone over <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> with vertex <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a general curve <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in{\mathcal{M}}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in{\mathcal{M}}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> we consider the linear system <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of quadratic sections of <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}=h^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{6},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{6}}(2))-h^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{5},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{5}}(2))-1=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>𝔏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}=h^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{6},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{6}}(2))-h^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{5},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{5}}(2))-1=6</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the incidence correspondence</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=\{(C,S)\ |\ C\subset S\}\subset|{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(2)|\times|{\mathcal{O}}_{\widehat{Y}}(2)|={\mathbb{P}}^{15}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{22}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>22</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=\{(C,S)\ |\ C\subset S\}\subset|{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(2)|\times|{\mathcal{O}}_{\widehat{Y}}(2)|={\mathbb{P}}^{15}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{22}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">together with the projection <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:I\to|{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(2)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:I\to|{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(2)|</annotation></semantics></math>, whose fibers are given by <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> has the structure of a <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle, whence <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(I)=15+6=21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(I)=15+6=21</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the proof of part (a) the general member of <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface in <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (note that <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P={\mathbb{P}}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P={\mathbb{P}}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{v}={\mathbb{P}}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{v}={\mathbb{P}}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=P\cap G(V_{5},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=P\cap G(V_{5},2)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Y}=\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}\cap P_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Y}=\widehat{G(V_{5},2)}\cap P_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> in the notation of that proof). Hence, we get a natural rational moduli map <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:I\dashrightarrow{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:I\dashrightarrow{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is unirational, the corollary will follow if we prove that <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is dominant, equivalently, generically finite, since <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible of the same dimension as <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume therefore that <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> has positive-dimensional fibers. Since the rational moduli map <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(2)|\dashrightarrow{\mathcal{M}}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{Y}(2)|\dashrightarrow{\mathcal{M}}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, the fibers of <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> lie in fibers of <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces in <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> do not have maximal variation in moduli.
Note that <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the quadratic sections of the form <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\cup Y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\cup Y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime}\in{\mathbb{P}}H^{0}(\widehat{Y},{\mathcal{O}}_{\widehat{Y}}(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime}\in{\mathbb{P}}H^{0}(\widehat{Y},{\mathcal{O}}_{\widehat{Y}}(1))</annotation></semantics></math> which form a hypersurface in <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence a general one-dimensional family in <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{L}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-isotrivial, a contradiction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(a) The proof of Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4.1. K3 sections of a cone of the Grassmannian 
          
            
              G
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    V
                    5
                  
                  ,
                  2
                  ) ‣ 4. K3 surfaces of genus 6 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> shows that our construction dominates the moduli space <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, the general <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface in <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quadratic section of a cone over a quintic Del Pezzo surface in <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(b) By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk93</a>]</cite>, all Brill–Noether general <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces of genus <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized as a quadratic section of either a smooth quintic Del Pezzo threefold in <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> or a cone over a quintic Del Pezzo surface. Item (a) shows that <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝔐</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{{\mathfrak{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the locus of <math id="S4.Thmdefinition4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces that cannot be realized in a smooth Del Pezzo threefold.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundles and their stability</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces constructed in Sections <a href="#S3" title="3. 
        
          
            ⁢
            K
            3
          
        
       surfaces of genus 
        
          4 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4" title="4. K3 surfaces of genus 6 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we will show that these are <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces without any stable rank <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> Lazarsfeld–Mukai bundle with determinant <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}=3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. This shows in particular that the result of Lelli-Chiesa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">LC13</a>,  Thm. 4.3]</cite> about stability of rank <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vector bundles on <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surfaces is optimal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We recall the definition and basic properties of Lazarsfeld-Mukai bundles, which will also be needed in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. K3 surfaces of genus 8 ‣ Brill–Noether general K3 surfaces with the maximal number of elliptic pencils of minimal degree" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Let <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface and let <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset S</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth curve of genus <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with a globally generated line bundle <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(C,A)=r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(C,A)=r+1</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lazarsfeld-Mukai bundle</em> <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined via
an elementary transformation on <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A}^{\vee}\longrightarrow H^{0}(C,A)\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{S}\longrightarrow A\longrightarrow 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\longrightarrow{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A}^{\vee}\longrightarrow H^{0}(C,A)\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{S}\longrightarrow A\longrightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is considered as a coherent sheaf on <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> supported on <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, it is a bundle of rank <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+1</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}({\mathcal{E}}_{C,A})=[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}({\mathcal{E}}_{C,A})=[C]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}({\mathcal{E}}_{C,A})=\deg A=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}({\mathcal{E}}_{C,A})=\deg A=d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(S,{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(S,{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The bundles have been introduced by Lazarsfeld <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laz86</a>]</cite> and Mukai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk89</a>]</cite>.
Dualizing the above sequence, we get</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\longrightarrow H^{0}(C,A)^{*}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{S}\longrightarrow{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A}\longrightarrow\omega_{C}\otimes A^{*}\longrightarrow 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\longrightarrow H^{0}(C,A)^{*}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{S}\longrightarrow{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A}\longrightarrow\omega_{C}\otimes A^{*}\longrightarrow 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and in particular a distinguished <math id="S5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace <math id="S5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(C,A)^{*}\subset H^{0}({\mathcal{E}}_{C,A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(C,A)^{*}\subset H^{0}({\mathcal{E}}_{C,A})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Apr13</a>, Prop. 1.3]</cite>, a rank <math id="S5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle <math id="S5.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a Lazarsfeld-Mukai bundle if and only if <math id="S5.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(S,{\mathcal{E}})=h^{2}(S,{\mathcal{E}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(S,{\mathcal{E}})=h^{2}(S,{\mathcal{E}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and there exists an <math id="S5.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace <math id="S5.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset H^{0}(S,{\mathcal{E}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset H^{0}(S,{\mathcal{E}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the degeneracy locus of the evaluation morphism <math id="S5.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{S}\to{\mathcal{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{S}\to{\mathcal{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth curve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in W^{1}_{d}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in W^{1}_{d}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq g-1</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by an elliptic pencil <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E|</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K3</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{C,A}</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-stable, where <math id="S5.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L={\mathcal{O}}_{S}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L={\mathcal{O}}_{S}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is essentially already contained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">AFO16</a>, Proof of Thm. 1.1]</cite>. Using the snake lemma, we get the following commutative diagram</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
